   image gate: house_1918_prairie chicagology_photo: verdict "differs", 6 difference(s) open: the camera itself is provisional (fitted by eye; see the camera's solve): through it the photograph's verandah and tower stand about 100-150 px (2.5-4 m) north of where the 1911 sheet puts them, and the photograph shows a bay of windows between the south-east corner and the verandah (x 1172-1395 px) that the build, laid on the sheet's plan, has no room for; re-solve with more points and settle the front's composition T-3330 tried: least-squares solves on the belvedere's top balustrade (its three posts read at x 1537, 1668 and 1735 px) with the house's corners (the south flank's west end at x 795, the south-east corner at x 1140, the north end at x 1825) do not close on the sheet's plan, 50-75 px rms whichever of the corners is the main block's and whichever is the reception bay's; the balustrade alone fits a camera about 100 m south-south-east on a long lens (5 px rms) if its tall post is the south-east corner, or one nearly due east if that post is the east face's middle, and neither agrees with the front's 685 px against the flank's 345. The Robinson atlas of 1886 (cgs-1936-robinson-1886) draws the tower in the middle of the front, about 6.6-10.4 m along a 14.2 m face, as the 1911 sheet does, and the etching (a19-cgy-1936-thompson-etching) draws the same order as the photograph: the corner, a two-window bay, the verandah (behind a tree), the tower, the stoop running north. So the front's composition is still the open question, now narrowed to where the photograph's corner at x 1140 stands on the plan.; the verandah south of the tower: the photograph's three arched openings on columns under a balustraded balcony (x 1395-1600, y 640-870 px). Its columns are built (T-3356): the two piers between the arches stand as fluted Corinthian shafts (x 1487-1505 and 1555-1568 px, capitals at y 708-722) on the solid end piers' line, so the openings read as an arcade on columns, not as holes in a wall. The dark rail across the openings' feet (y 822 px, about 0.8 m over the floor) is built (T-3357): a 0.06 m iron bar from jamb to column across each opening at 0.8 m, on one standard in its middle, as the photograph's first two openings show it (x about 1460 and 1527 px). Still open: its place along the front (see the camera item); the entrance porch at the tower's north-east foot and its stoop running north along the front (x 1655-1950, y 700-960 px): built as a columned hood over a straight stoop toward the avenue, beside the tower rather than in it (K01 refuses a door behind a tower); the window dressings: the photograph's pedimented and scrolled brownstone hoods, the paired principal windows and the round-headed chamber lights on the south flank; built as K09's corniced lintels over flat-headed sashes; the chimney stacks: the photograph's three tall panelled stacks with heavy caps, the left one over the flank's middle (x 905-995, top y 270 px) and the right one over the front part (x 1215-1290, top y 185 px), stand taller and further east than the build's (T-3330 raised them 1.22 m with the cornice and the deck; their places wait on the camera); the roof's deck stands higher in the photograph (its rail at y 260-300 px over the south-east corner) than the build's 15.0 m (T-3330 raised it with the main cornice, 15.0 to 16.22 m; how it stands against the photograph waits on the camera)
